spread=add('lem-local-normalized-point-blowup-sequences-spread-at-closed-points','Local normalized point sequences spread at closed surface points',['def-normal-surface-modification-and-normalized-point-blowup','lem-surface-finite-type-normalization-finite','thm-blowup-base-change-flat','thm-integrality-commutes-with-localisation','lem-relative-spec-glues-affine-algebras'],r'''Let $X$ be a normal integral surface locally of finite type over a permitted base and $x\in X$ a closed point with two-dimensional local ring $B$. Any finite normalized point-blowup sequence over $\operatorname{Spec}B$ spreads to the same finite sequence of normalized blowups at closed points of $X$, unchanged off $x$. Its localization over $\operatorname{Spec}B$ is the given sequence. If $X$ is projective over a Noetherian affine base, so is the spread sequence.''',r'''Each centre of a proper scheme over the local base B lies on its closed fibre: the image of a closed point is closed under the proper map. Its residue field is finite over κ(x), since that fibre is finite type over κ(x). Start by blowing up x globally. Localization is flat, so its blowup localizes to the local blowup by the actual flat blowup-base-change supplier; normalization commutes with localization because the affine integral closures are computed in the common function field. The local centre of the next step is a point on the global model's fibre over x. Its finite residue field makes it a closed point of that finite-type fibre, hence of the global scheme since x is closed. Blow it up globally and normalize finitely; continue inductively. Away from x these are isomorphisms. Projectivity is preserved by twisting the centre ideal by an ample bundle and taking a finite generating family, followed by finite-over-projective normalization, exactly as in the normalized-point domination helper. The sequence is finite, so no limit or arbitrary-modification spreading theorem is used.''')
rows[spread]['deps']=sorted(set(rows[spread]['deps']+['lem-finite-over-projective-noetherian-affine-base-is-projective','lem-eventual-global-generation-coherent-twists']))
propagate=add('lem-rational-surface-local-rings-propagate-by-point-sequence-spreading','Rationality propagates to birational local surface rings',['def-rational-normal-surface-singularity-and-bounded-modification-h1',spread,'lem-normal-surface-modification-leray-short-exact-sequence','lem-surface-finite-type-normalization-finite','lem-local-normal-surface-modification-dimension-and-projective-cohomology','lem-finite-over-projective-noetherian-affine-base-is-projective'],r'''If a permitted normal local surface domain $A$ is rational and $A\subset B$ is a normal two-dimensional local domain with the same fraction field, essentially of finite type over $A$, then $B$ is rational. If modification H1 over $A$ is uniformly bounded, some finite normalized point-blowup sequence over $A$ has rational local rings at every closed point of its terminal surface.''',r'''Write B=C_q with C a finite-type A-domain in their common function field. Take its affine projective closure and finite normalization. The resulting normal projective modification X contains the same normal local ring B at a point x: normalization localizes to B, so it has exactly one point above q and leaves its local ring unchanged. The integral modification has dimension two by the dimension helper; local dimension two forces x closed (a further strict specialization would give a chain of length at least three). To test rationality of B, it suffices by normalized-point domination to test its normalized point sequences. The spreading helper extends each such sequence to X′→X. Rationality of A makes H1(X′,O)=0. The Leray exact sequence on projective X then makes R1g*O=0. Localization/Čech base change identifies its stalk at x with the tested H1 over B, so it vanishes. For bounded H1 choose a normal projective modification maximizing its integer length; such a maximum exists since the set is nonempty and bounded in N. Dominate it by a normalized point sequence. Leray injection and maximality show the terminal scheme still attains the maximum. Any further normal projective modification of this scheme has the same H1 length and hence R1g*O=0. Spread the point sequences at each closed local ring as above and use localization; they all have zero H1. The point-sequence test proves those local rings rational. No general arbitrary-modification extension or limits theorem is imported.''')
powers=add('lem-rational-surface-exceptional-ideal-powers-and-sections','Powers and sections of a rational surface exceptional ideal',['def-rational-normal-surface-singularity-and-bounded-modification-h1','lem-local-normal-surface-modification-dimension-and-projective-cohomology','thm-long-exact-sequence-sheaf-cohomology','def-globally-generated-sheaf'],r'''Let $A$ be a rational permitted normal local surface domain and $X\to\operatorname{Spec}A$ a normal projective modification. A coherent globally generated sheaf $F$ on $X$ has $H^1(X,F)=0$. If the scheme-theoretic closed fibre is Cartier with ideal $I=\mathfrak m\mathcal O_X$, then $H^0(X,I^n)=\mathfrak m^n$ and $H^1(X,I^n)=0$ for all $n\ge0$.''',r'''A coherent globally generated sheaf on the quasi-compact X admits a finite global generating family, by taking finite generators on a finite cover; hence O_X^r→F is onto. Its kernel is coherent, and H2 of that kernel is zero by the two-affine cover. Rationality gives H1(O)=0, so the long exact sequence gives H1(F)=0. Every I^n is globally generated by the finite products of generators of m, hence has zero H1. H0(I)=m: H0(O)=A and its restriction to the nonempty closed fibre has kernel m, since a unit remains a unit there and every element of m vanishes. Let x1,...,xr generate I and let K be the kernel of O^r→I. Locally one generator is a unit in a frame of I. The Koszul relations xi e_j−xj e_i therefore generate K⊗I (in frames this is the kernel of a row with a unit entry, whose Koszul relations generate); these relations are global images of ∧²O^r. Thus K⊗I is globally generated. Tensoring with the globally generated I^(n−2) shows K⊗I^(n−1) globally generated for n≥2, hence H1 zero. The sequence 0→K⊗I^(n−1)→(I^(n−1))^r→I^n→0 gives surjection on H0. Induction yields H0(I^n)=Σ xi m^(n−1)=m^n. The n=0 case is H0(O)=A and rationality.''')
blow=add('lem-rational-normal-surface-point-blowup-normal-and-fibre-cohomology','Normality and fibre cohomology of a rational surface point blowup',['def-rational-normal-surface-singularity-and-bounded-modification-h1',powers,'lem-surface-finite-type-normalization-finite','lem-finite-over-projective-noetherian-affine-base-is-projective','thm-serre-vanishing','lem-eventual-global-generation-coherent-twists','thm-pullback-center-ideal-invertible','lem-normal-domain-implies-s-two','cor-regular-quotient-cohen-macaulay-equivalence','def-degree-invertible-sheaf-proper-dimension-one','def-embedding-dimension-and-regular-local-ring'],r'''For a rational permitted normal local surface domain $(A,\mathfrak m,\kappa)$, its ordinary point blowup $X$ is normal. Its exceptional fibre $E$ is a projective pure CM curve, its tautological conormal line $L=\mathcal O_E(1)$ is very ample, and $H^1(E,L^n)=0$, $H^0(E,L^n)=\mathfrak m^n/\mathfrak m^{n+1}$ for $n\ge0$. In particular $H^0(E,O)=\kappa$ and $\deg_\kappa L=\dim_\kappa(\mathfrak m/\mathfrak m^2)-1$, which is at least one and equals one only for regular $A$.''',r'''Let X0 be the ordinary blowup and ν:X→X0 its finite normalization. The point ideal pulls back to invertible I′=O_X0(1), with pullback I on X; both exceptional divisors are Cartier since the schemes are integral and the centre ideal is nonzero. The preceding powers lemma gives H0(X,I^n)=m^n. There are injections m^n→H0(X0,(I′)^n)→H0(X,I^n), whose composite is the identity inclusion in the common fraction field, so both are equalities. If Q=ν*O_X/O_X0 were nonzero, then Q(n) for n≫0 would be globally generated and nonzero, so would have a nonzero global section. Serre vanishing makes H1(X0,(I′)^n)=0, and the finite projection formula makes H0 of the middle sheaf equal to H0(X,I^n)=m^n. The long exact sequence would then give H0(Q(n))=0, contradiction. Thus ν is an isomorphism and X0 is normal. Normal two-dimensional rings are CM, and their nonzerodivisor Cartier quotients are pure CM dimension one, giving E. Apply the powers lemma to 0→I^(n+1)→I^n→O_E(n)→0. H1 of both powers and H2 of the kernel vanish, giving the two fibre cohomology formulas. The blowup embedding gives very ampleness. Euler-characteristic degree is μ−1, with μ=dimκ m/m²; regularity is precisely μ=dim A=2.''')
